export default {"id":"bassstation-level-15","number":15,"title":"32-Step Pattern Sequencer","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Master the dual silkscreen layout (black-on-white second functions) governing the onboard 32-step sequencer. - Select and program across all four non-volatile sequence memory banks (SEQ 1, SEQ 2, SEQ 3, SEQ 4).","modules":[{"id":"bassstation-m-15-1","code":"15","title":"32-Step Pattern Sequencer","blurb":"- Master the dual silkscreen layout (black-on-white second functions) governing the onboard 32-step sequencer. - Select and program across all four non-volatile sequence memory banks (SEQ 1, SEQ 2, SEQ 3, SEQ 4).","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"bassstation-l-o-15","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 15.1 — Step Sequencer Architecture, Programming & Playback","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Master the dual silkscreen layout (black-on-white second functions) governing the onboard 32-step sequencer.","Select and program across all four non-volatile sequence memory banks (<strong>SEQ 1</strong>, <strong>SEQ 2</strong>, <strong>SEQ 3</strong>, <strong>SEQ 4</strong>).","Execute step recording routines, inserting discrete musical pitches, <strong>Rests</strong>, and <strong>Legato / Ties</strong>.","Perform real-time sequence playback and dynamic root-key transposition.","Explain the interaction between recorded step sequences and the 32 <strong>Rhythm</strong> groove templates.","Calibrate the <strong>SEQ Retrig</strong> On-Key function for multi-bar phrase management.","Understand non-volatile sequence storage rules across global memory and individual patch saves."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-15-1-1","title":"15.1.1: The Shared Silkscreen Legend and Sequencer Mode Selection","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Bass Station II sequencer shares physical hardware controls with the Arpeggiator, utilizing high-contrast <strong>black-on-white inverted silkscreen legends</strong> to denote sequencer functions:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    ARP / SEQUENCER DUAL-FUNCTION MAP                       │\n├───────────────────┬────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┤\n│ Physical Control  │ White Silkscreen (Arp Mode)│ Black-on-White (Seq Mode) │\n├───────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤\n│ Top Left Button   │ [On]                       │ [Legato] (Inserts Tie)    │\n│ Top Right Button  │ [Latch]                    │ [Rest] (Inserts Pause)    │\n│ Mode Rotary Dial  │ Up / Down / UpDn / Random  │ [Record] &amp; [Play]         │\n│ Octaves / SEQ Dial│ 1, 2, 3, 4 Octaves         │ [SEQ 1, 2, 3, 4] Memories │\n└───────────────────┴────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Entering Sequencer Mode</strong>:","Rotate the <strong>Mode</strong> rotary switch to <strong>Record</strong> to program steps, or to <strong>Play</strong> to execute sequence playback.","When set to Record or Play, the sequencer engine supersedes standard arpeggiator logic."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-15-1-2","title":"15.1.2: Selecting Sequence Memory Banks (SEQ 1–4)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Bass Station II contains <strong>four global, non-volatile sequence memory registers</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["While the <strong>Mode</strong> dial is set to <strong>Record</strong> or <strong>Play</strong>, the adjacent <strong>Octaves / SEQ</strong> rotary switch selects the target memory bank: <strong>SEQ 1</strong>, <strong>SEQ 2</strong>, <strong>SEQ 3</strong>, or <strong>SEQ 4</strong>.","Each of the four memory banks can store an independent sequence of up to <strong>32 discrete steps</strong>.","Sequences stored in memory banks 1–4 are preserved across power cycles."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-15-1-3","title":"15.1.3: Step Recording Workflow (Up to 32 Steps)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The sequencer operates via a straightforward step-entry protocol:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      STEP SEQUENCER RECORDING ROUTINE\n   [Step 1] Set Mode to [Record] ──► Display Flashes `rEC` then Shows ` 1`\n   [Step 2] Select Target Bank [SEQ 1, 2, 3, or 4]\n   [Step 3] Step Entry Sequence:\n              ├── Strike Keybed Note ──► Enters Pitch (Step Advances)\n              ├── Press [Rest]       ──► Enters Silent Step (Step Advances)\n              └── Press [Legato]     ──► Enters Tie (Step Advances)\n   [Step 4] Rotate Mode to [Play] ──► Finalizes &amp; Saves Sequence into Memory</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Recording Procedure</strong>:","Set Mode to <strong>Record</strong>. The display momentarily flashes <code>rEC</code>, then displays step number <code> 1</code>.","Striking any key on the keybed writes that pitch into the active step and automatically increments the step counter to <code> 2</code>.","Enter subsequent notes sequentially up to a maximum of <strong>32 steps</strong>.","Shorter sequences (e.g., 8, 12, or 16 steps) can be finalized at any time simply by turning the <strong>Mode</strong> switch away from Record (e.g., to <strong>Play</strong>)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Mode to <strong>Record</strong> and select <strong>SEQ 1</strong>.","Play the notes: <code>C3</code>, <code>D#3</code>, <code>F3</code>, <code>G3</code>, <code>A#3</code>, <code>C4</code>.","Observe the step display advance from <code> 1</code> to <code> 7</code>.","Turn Mode to <strong>Play</strong>. Strike Middle C; hear your 6-note sequence loop cleanly."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-15-1-4","title":"15.1.4: Programming Rests and Legato Ties","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To program dynamic phrasing, syncopation, and sustained notes:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        REST AND TIE STEP ENTRY                             │\n├───────────────┬───────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Step Type     │ Button Pressed            │ Display Readout &amp; Behavior     │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Note Step     │ Strike Keybed Note        │ Step increments (` 1`, ` 2`...)│\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Rest Step     │ Press [Latch / Rest]      │ Silent pause inserted; step    │\n│               │                           │ advances to next position.     │\n├───────────────┼───────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Legato / Tie  │ Press [On / Legato]       │ Display shows a dash (`-`);    │\n│               │                           │ ties previous note into step.  │\n└───────────────┴───────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Rest Entry</strong>:","Press the <strong>Rest</strong> button to insert a silent pause at the active step position.","<strong>Legato / Tie Entry</strong>:","To hold a note across multiple steps, strike the note on Step 1, then press <strong>Legato</strong> on Step 2.","The display confirms the tie with a center dash (<code>-</code>).","The note sustains continuously across both steps without re-triggering the envelope.","<em>Rule</em>: You cannot tie into a rest step."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Mode to <strong>Record</strong>.","Step 1: Strike <code>C3</code>.","Step 2: Press <strong>Legato</strong> (display shows <code>-</code>).","Step 3: Press <strong>Rest</strong>.","Step 4: Strike <code>G3</code>.","Switch Mode to <strong>Play</strong>; listen to the rhythm: Long C (2 steps), Rest (1 step), Short G (1 step)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-15-1-5","title":"15.1.5: Playback, Latch, and Real-Time Transposition","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Playback Triggering</strong>:","Rotate Mode to <strong>Play</strong>.","Pressing and holding any key on the keybed launches sequence playback.","<strong>Dynamic Root Transposition</strong>:","The sequencer uses the <strong>first key struck during Play mode as the root reference pitch</strong>.","Striking a higher or lower key immediately transposes the entire sequence in real time, preserving all relative pitch intervals.","<strong>Continuous Latch</strong>:","Press the <strong>Latch</strong> button while a sequence is playing. You can release your hands; the sequence loops indefinitely. Striking a new key transposes the running loop instantly."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-15-1-6","title":"15.1.6: The Rhythm Template Interaction","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"An essential architectural characteristic of the Bass Station II sequencer is that <strong>step notes do not contain hardcoded rhythmic lengths in memory</strong>."},{"type":"list","items":["The timing, velocity accents, and gate durations of sequence playback are governed entirely by the <strong>Rhythm</strong> rotary switch (Patterns <code>1</code> to <code>32</code>).","Setting Rhythm to <code>1</code> plays the sequence in straight, even clock divisions.","Selecting Rhythms <code>2</code> through <code>32</code> imposes complex syncopated grooves, shuffles, and velocity dynamics on top of your recorded pitch sequence."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["With your recorded sequence looping in <strong>Play</strong> mode, slowly rotate the <strong>Rhythm</strong> switch from <code>1</code> to <code>15</code>.","Hear how the exact same sequence of pitches transforms dynamically into various funk, dubstep, and broken-beat grooves."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-15-1-7","title":"15.1.7: SEQ Retrig Configuration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Operational Logic</strong>:","<strong><code>SEQ Retrig On</code> (Default)</strong>: The sequence automatically resets to Step 1 at the beginning of every 2-bar cycle (every 32 sixteenth-note clocks), aligning playback to standard 4/4 musical bar structures.","<strong><code>SEQ Retrig OFF</code></strong>: The sequencer ignores 2-bar boundaries, playing through all recorded steps continuously to completion. Essential for odd-meter patterns (e.g., 7-step, 11-step, or 15-step polyrhythmic loops) that must drift across the bar line.","<strong>On-Key Activation</strong>:","Press <strong>Function / Exit</strong>, then press the <strong>SEQ Retrig</strong> key (the upper <code>G</code> key).","Display indicates <code>On</code> or <code>OFF</code>."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-15-1-8","title":"15.1.8: Non-Volatile Sequence Storage Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Global Persistence</strong>:","The four sequence memories (SEQ 1–4) reside in non-volatile EEPROM and remain preserved when the synthesizer is powered off.","<strong>Patch Association</strong>:","When a sound patch (0–127) is saved, the <strong>currently active SEQ memory number (1, 2, 3, or 4)</strong> is saved as a parameter of that patch.","Loading that patch automatically selects its associated sequence memory."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
